<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milton Obote</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milton Obote was Uganda's first prime minister at independence in 1962 and later its president. In 1966 he suspended the constitution and sent troops against the palace of the kabaka of Buganda, Mutesa II, and in 1967 he abolished the kingdoms. Overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> in 1971, he returned to power in 1980 and was deposed again in 1985.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milton-obote.jpg" alt="Milton Obote" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Warren K. Leffler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_John_F._Kennedy_with_Prime_Minister_Milton_Obote_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Apollo Milton Obote was born on 28 December 1925 in the village of Akokoro in northern Uganda, the third of nine children of a chief of the Oyima clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lango">Lango</a> people. He studied at Busoga College Mwiri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makerere-university">Makerere University</a>, where he took a general arts course because law was not taught, and left without a degree. He worked in Buganda and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, where he was involved in the independence movement. After returning to Uganda in 1956 he joined the Uganda National Congress and was elected to the colonial legislative council in 1957. In 1959 he led a faction into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-congress">Uganda People's Congress</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before independence Obote formed a coalition with the Buganda royalist party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a>, and he became prime minister on 25 April 1962; Mutesa II, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka of Buganda</a>, became ceremonial president. In January 1964 the army mutinied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinja">Jinja</a>, and a settlement gave soldiers large pay rises and rapid promotions, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>. Obote was later implicated in a gold-smuggling plot with Amin. When Parliament demanded an investigation, he suspended the constitution in March 1966 and declared himself president, and an attack on Mutesa's palace sent Mutesa into exile. A new constitution in 1967 abolished the federal structure and created an executive presidency.</p><p>After an assassination attempt on 19 December 1969 all opposition parties were banned. His Common Man's Charter set out his Move to the Left, and in 1970 the state took a 60 percent share in major companies and banks. His secret police, the General Service Unit, was held responsible for many cruelties. The army under Amin overthrew him in January 1971 while he attended a Commonwealth conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Obote went into exile in Tanzania, where President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> supported him, and a rebel invasion of Uganda in 1972 failed. After Tanzanian forces and Ugandan exiles ousted Amin in 1979, his party won the elections of 1980, which its opponents believed were rigged, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>. In 1983 Operation Bonanza killed tens of thousands of people. Estimates of the deaths in the fighting range from 100,000 to 500,000. Army commanders deposed Obote on 27 July 1985, and he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. He died of kidney failure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> on 10 October 2005, aged 79, and was given a state funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milton Obote” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the 1966 crisis between Prime Minister <b>Milton Obote</b> and Buganda, the republican constitution of 1967 abolished all of Uganda's kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At independence in 1962 Buganda received federal status, and its party, Kabaka Yekka, allied with <b>Milton Obote</b>’s Uganda People’s Congress; Mutesa II became president of Uganda in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The colonial settlement left a lasting grievance, the 'lost counties' transferred to Buganda in the 1890s; the referendum of 1964 that returned two of them to Bunyoro contributed to the crisis between <b>Milton Obote</b> and Buganda that ended with the abolition of the kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Milton Obote</b>, leader of the Uganda People’s Congress, became prime minister through an alliance with the Buganda royalist movement Kabaka Yekka, defeating the Democratic Party of Benedicto Kiwanuka, who had led the government during the brief period of internal self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>While President <b>Milton Obote</b> was abroad, army commander Idi Amin took power in a coup.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Uganda became independent with <b>Milton Obote</b> as prime minister and the Kabaka of Buganda later as president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1977" aria-label="Johannesburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Milton Obote"><image href="/maps/globes/g030s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 4677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 4677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1977">What was here in 1977? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
